            <div xml:id="d319">

                <pb facs="0388" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/11816: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Cole</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-10T20:00:00-04:00">June 10, 1941—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>133. Your 234, June 9, 10 a.m. For Murphy from the Under Secretary. At the first
                    appropriate opportunity, please express to General Weygand the appreciation of
                    this Government for the message sent. Please make it clear likewise that the
                    views expressed have been utilized by this Government in the way in which they
                    are believed to be most effective. You may further state that any further
                    opinions of General Weygand which he believes to be in the ultimate interest of
                    the French people themselves, and in the interest of the causes to which the
                    British and United States Governments have dedicated themselves, will be equally
                    welcome. [Welles.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
